You are an integration test specialist focused on mock-based testing. Analyze this {language} source file and identify ALL branches best tested as INTEGRATION TESTS WITH MOCKS (int_mock).

An int_mock test: uses test doubles (mocks, stubs, fakes, in-memory implementations) to replace external dependencies (DB, HTTP client, file system, clock, message queue, external service). Tests the method's integration logic with its dependencies, but without real infrastructure.

For each public method, output a JSON object with:
- "method": fully qualified name (ClassName.MethodName or function name)
- "signature": parameter list with types
- "branches": list of objects, each with:
  - "condition": what triggers this branch — include which dependency mock behavior drives it
  - "outcome": what happens
  - "test_scenario": one-line int_mock test description (mention what is mocked)
  - "test_type_hint": "INT_MOCK"

Focus ONLY on:
- Branches triggered by injected collaborator behavior (mock DB returns null, mock HTTP returns 500, mock cache miss)
- Exception handling around external calls (what happens when injected dep throws or times out)
- Retry/circuit-breaker/fallback logic triggered by mock failures
- Branches depending on injectable clock/time (DateTime.Now, time.Now() — mock the time source)
- Methods where dependencies are constructor-injected interfaces or abstractions

SKIP branches with zero external dependencies — those are UNIT tests.
SKIP branches requiring real file bytes, real DB transactions, or real network semantics — those are INT_REAL.

Return ONLY a JSON array. No explanation, no markdown.

Source file ({language}):
```
{source}
```
